questionText:
Real code we built:

public abstract class PaymentDecorator implements IPayment {

    protected IPayment ip;

    public PaymentDecorator(IPayment ip) {
        this.ip = ip;
    }
}

Why is ip declared protected instead of private, given PaymentDecorator itself never calls ip.pay()?

Given:

IPayment upiWithFeeThenReward = new RewardDecorator(new FeeDecorator(new UPI()));
upiWithFeeThenReward.pay();

with FeeDecorator.pay() calling ip.pay() first, then printing "deducting fee", and RewardDecorator.pay() calling ip.pay() first, then printing "adding reward points" — what's the exact printed order?

In the coffee shop version, MilkDecorator.cost() would look like:

public double cost() {
    return beverage.cost() + 0.50;
}

Why does it call beverage.cost() rather than hardcoding the base Espresso price directly inside MilkDecorator?

With strategies mixed in:

IPayment cardWithFee = new FeeDecorator(new Card(), new CardFeeStrategy());
IPayment upiWithFee = new FeeDecorator(new UPI(), new UpiFeeStrategy());

If someone accidentally writes new FeeDecorator(new Card(), new UpiFeeStrategy()) — mismatching the payment type and the strategy — does this cause a compile error?
